  "assessment_summary": "This paper presents a survey of 51 experts on AGI safety and governance practices, finding broad consensus across 50 statements. While the work addresses an important policy-relevant topic and achieves a strong response rate (55.4%), it faces several methodological limitations. The sampling approach, while purposive and well-justified for this specialized field, is small (N=51) and may suffer from selection bias toward experts already engaged with AI safety discourse. The survey design uses abstract statements that may inflate agreement levels compared to more specific implementation details. Statistical power is limited for subgroup analyses, and while Mann-Whitney U and Chi-squared tests are appropriate, multiple comparison corrections reduce the likelihood of detecting real differences. The response scale and demographic categorizations are reasonable, though the binary treatment of complex governance practices may oversimplify nuanced positions. The paper's transparency is commendable, with pre-registration and open materials, though some deviations from the pre-analysis plan occurred. The cluster analysis was unsuccessful, likely due to sample size limitations. While the findings provide valuable input for policy discussions, the high agreement levels (85.2% average agreement) may reflect either genuine consensus or methodological artifacts. The work would benefit from follow-up studies with larger, more diverse samples and more concrete implementation scenarios. The practical applications for AGI labs, regulators, and standard-setting bodies are clearly articulated, though the translation from abstract agreement to actual implementation remains challenging.",

